ops.description:
Paint operations, applied in order. Two shapes:
  {"op":"path","terrain":"water","points":[[x,y],...],"width":3}
  {"op":"rect","terrain":"grass","x":10,"y":4,"width":8,"height":6}
Use terrain "none" to erase. Coordinates are terrain cells, as shown by get_map, and may be negative. An empty list paints nothing and only attaches library tilesets that blend two of this map's terrains, painted or not.

Tile operations, applied in order. Three shapes:
  {"op":"place","layer":0,"tile":5,"cells":[[q,r],...],"level":0,"stack":1}
  {"op":"erase","layer":0,"area":{"q":0,"r":0,"width":4,"height":3}}
  {"op":"add_group","tile_group_id":"<uuid>"}
layer: tile layer index from get_map. tile: tile index in that layer's tile group (tile_N in get_tiles_pro). cells or area picks where; area covers q..q+width-1, r..r+height-1. level: stack level, 0 = ground, up to 63. stack: how many levels to fill upward from there. "on_top": true instead of level puts each tile one level above whatever that cell already holds; on erase it peels the top `stack` tiles off. Erasing at a level removes everything stacked above it too. add_group lays another tile group (list_tiles_pro) on the map as new layers after the existing ones; later ops in the same call can use them. It needs a map made from tile groups (create_map(tile_group_id=...)); a map painted with terrain tilesets takes one only in Map Workshop (Add tile group).

tools.description:
Ask for help using PixelLab MCP tools, workflows, or features.
A knowledge agent searches our docs and returns a concise answer;
tool="<name>" returns that tool's full reference directly.
